> The following is a box included in a report I am writing:
> ===
> Box 2. Identifying private household resilience to guide ‘easy win’ entry points for public investment in climate resilience: Kenya Case Study
> 
> Awareness of the El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) is widespread, but the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) has only recently gained attention after its severe impacts in East Africa. The IOD is a climate phenomenon marked by shifts in sea surface temperatures between the western and eastern Indian Ocean, classified as negative or positive, which influence rainfall and weather in Africa, Asia, and Australia. In a positive IOD (pIOD), warmer waters in the western Indian Ocean and cooler waters near Indonesia bring heavy rains to East Africa and drought to parts of Australia and Southeast Asia. Moreover, climate change is increasing the frequency of pIOD events, while intensifying hazards which disproportionately affect resource-poor household (REFERENCE).
> The 2019 pIOD drew attention for triggering variable rainfall, cyclones, floods, and Desert Locust outbreaks. Building resilience to pIOD risks requires actors at all levels to understand available options and their effectiveness. Current focus on infrastructure and mortality overlooks wider impacts, risking misallocation of resources. This report draws on World Bank household surveys (2019–2022) to assess hazard impacts and coping mechanisms. The goal is to guide investment priorities by identifying pIOD impacts, evaluating adaptation and coping strategies, and exploring opportunities to strengthen generalized resilience.
> Burden on public budget of complex pIOD risk
> Recovery from the impacts of the 2019 pIOD event East Africa are estimated between $1–9 billion (World Bank, 2020). Vulnerable groups already facing undernutrition, disease, and food price volatility have been hit hardest (Serdeczny et al., 2017). Kenya’s Social Safety Net Programme has been vital, but this analysis finds that government aid spending rose more than forty-fold across survey waves, creating major fiscal pressures (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming).
> Spending increases were driven by expanded coverage rather than larger transfers: average benefits per household stayed between 2,100–3,400 Ksh, while coverage rose from <1% to 11% before dipping to 9%. Aid per 100 households peaked at nearly 28,000 Ksh before contracting, reflecting both welfare gains and fiscal risks, including crowding out of health, education, and infrastructure while risking increased public debt.
> Aid distribution showed a strong agricultural bias. Agricultural households received more total and per-household aid than non-agricultural ones, with coverage peaking at 12% versus 3%. This targeted support to rural and food-producing households likely enhanced food security but raises vulnerability concerns and concentrated fiscal strain in one sector.
> Preliminary results of regressions undertaken for a forthcoming paper, reveal mixed targeting (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming). Among agricultural workers, farmers, and pastoralists, aid often aligned with food insecurity, loan reliance, and child labour, but wealthier households—those with better housing or electricity—also received disproportionate support. Pastoralists appeared better targeted, with lower risks of elite capture.
> While aid helps shield vulnerable groups, aid alone risks fiscal strain. Strengthening vulnerability-based criteria and institutionalizing spending are key to balancing welfare protection with fiscal sustainability.
> Investment requirements to boost resilience
>  To reduce the fiscal burden of reactive aid and strengthen resilience, investments must address hazard, vulnerability, and response determinants of pIOD risks. The 2019 pIOD exposed Kenyan farmers to multiple hazards. Floods damaged infrastructure, disrupted markets, and reduced yields, while locusts harmed crops and grazing lands, worsened by pesticide shortages and unsafe control practices. Results show exposure is shaped by livelihood, crop choice, and infrastructure. Potato and cassava growers are more flood-prone while banana and kale growers are less exposed to locusts. Electricity access is linked to higher locust exposure in agriculturally active zones—perhaps driven by greater capacity for higher yields that attract locusts.
> Vulnerability depends on local government budgets, preparedness, wealth, and income diversity. Wealth enables recovery and diversification, while weak budgets limit safety net capacity. Social safety nets help but are often underfunded. Pre-existing poverty forces households to buy food when harvests fail. Results demonstrate that farm size is the strongest predictor of earnings, followed by household size and housing quality. Access to fertilizer and credit boosts incomes, but reliance on informal loans often signals distress.
> With regards to how agricultural workers respond, farmers benefit most from land, fertilizer, and formal finance, while pastoralist incomes depend on child labor, credit, and government aid. Wealth indicators improve earnings for both groups. Building resilience requires crop- and livelihood-specific interventions, better credit and input access, stronger safety nets, and sustainable budget frameworks.
> Added benefits of investments
> Investing in climate resilience for Kenyan agricultural households provides benefits beyond fiscal savings, including avoided losses and co-benefits.
> Climate shocks damage infrastructure, disrupt markets, and strain resources, leaving communities with few options: rebuilding, shifting livelihoods, or displacement. Displacement often leads to permanent migration and uncultivated land, worsening desertification and flood risk. Desert Locust outbreaks add further risks, forcing trade-offs between crop destruction and hazardous pesticide spraying.
> Resilience investments reduce hazard exposure and food insecurity while strengthening welfare. Results demonstrate that floods are a consistent driver of hunger across agricultural workers, farmers, and pastoralists. Hunger is also linked to weak assets, poor access to seeds or fertilizer, and reliance on reactive coping mechanisms—credit and child labour. Better housing, electricity, inputs, and food expenditures lower hunger risks. Expanding adaptive credit, safety nets, and resilient inputs reduces reliance on loans and child labour while stabilizing welfare.
> Resilience investments yield three key benefits—(1) avoiding welfare and food security losses, (2) reducing harmful coping mechanisms, and (3) saving fiscally by lowering emergency aid needs—while also mitigating government liabilities from loan defaults and large-scale bailouts.
> Policy recommendations
> Tailored strategies are essential for optimal use of resources. Farmers need improved productivity through land access, inputs, and credit, while pastoralists require stronger financial networks, targeted aid, and stable food access. Based on the evidence presented, the following policy recommendations are proposed to strengthen resilience while ensuring fiscal sustainability:
> • Institutionalize climate-related aid spending within a medium-term expenditure framework to reduce fiscal volatility and ensure predictability.
> • Expand early warning systems and pest surveillance to manage floods, droughts, and locust outbreaks proactively.
> • Strengthen local government fiscal capacity and planning to improve emergency preparedness and reduce reliance on central government bailouts.
> • Invest in resilient infrastructure, including irrigation, flood defences, and rural roads, to mitigate hazard impacts and improve market access.
> • Scale up social safety nets and adaptive credit mechanisms to reduce reliance on harmful coping strategies such as child labour and emergency loans.
> • Improve access to agricultural inputs to boost productivity and resilience of smallholder farmers.
> • Diversify rural livelihoods through education, skills development, and financial inclusion initiatives to reduce vulnerability to single-shock dependence.
> ===
